","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.214029,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.313846,"nucleus_score":-2.11605,"pct_ad":0.1719,"pct_control":0.203831,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.940938,"donor_log2fc":-0.280637,"donor_lfcse":0.179776,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00177143},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"GLUD1","gene_symbol_current":"GLUD1","gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.0136001,"nucleus_log2fc":0.179308,"nucleus_score":2.81521,"pct_ad":0.102804,"pct_control":0.0901178,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.90387,"donor_log2fc":0.0770125,"donor_lfcse":0.109506,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00588182}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","module":"green","kme":0.321055,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.32433,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","module":"turquoise","kme":0.180136,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.572565,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","module":"brown","kme":0.191679,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.372712,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","module":"turquoise","kme":0.180203,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.704234,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000148672","entrez_id":["2746"],"uniprot":["P00367"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene encodes glutamate dehydrogenase, which is a mitochondrial matrix enzyme that catalyzes the oxidative deamination of glutamate to alpha-ketoglutarate and ammonia. This enzyme has an important role in regulating amino acid-induced insulin secretion. It is allosterically activated by ADP and inhibited by GTP and ATP. Activating mutations in this gene are a common cause of congenital hyperinsulinism. Alternative splicing of this gene results in multiple transcript variants. The related glutamate dehydrogenase 2 gene on the human X-chromosome originated from this gene via retrotransposition and encodes a soluble form of glutamate dehydrogenase. Related pseudogenes have been identified on chromosomes 10, 18 and X. [provided by RefSeq, Jan 2016].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P00367","text":"Mitochondrial glutamate dehydrogenase that catalyzes the conversion of L-glutamate into alpha-ketoglutarate. Plays a key role in glutamine anaplerosis by producing alpha-ketoglutarate, an important intermediate in the tricarboxylic acid cycle (PubMed:11032875, PubMed:11254391, PubMed:16023112, PubMed:16959573). Plays a role in insulin homeostasis (PubMed:11297618, PubMed:9571255). May be involved in learning and memory reactions by increasing the turnover of the excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate (By similarity)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}